 "bloques": [
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "I have to.",
   "texto": [
    "«We have to» or «I have to» is used to say **нужно** or **надо** + dative + infinitive: **Мне нужно идти** (I have to go), **Тебе надо отдохнуть** (you have to rest).",
    "In this construction, **нужно** and **надо** express need and are followed by infinitive.",
    "**Должен / должна / должны** + infinitive expresses a personal obligation and matches in gender and number with the subject in nominee: **Я должен идти** (speaks a man), **Я должна идти** (speaks a woman), **Мы должны идти** (plural).",
    "**Придётся** + infinitive expresses a necessity that is imposed: **Мне придётся идти** (I will have to go)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Can do something",
   "texto": [
    "**Мочь** means «poder» in the sense of having capacity or possibility and is conjugated: **я могу, ты можешь, он/она может, мы можем, вы можете, они могут**. It follows an infinitive: **Я могу прийти** (I can come). **Можно** + infinitive expresses permission or general possibility; **нельзя** expresses that something is not allowed."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Can / cannot",
   "texto": [
    "**Можно** + infinitive is used to ask for permission: **Можно войти?** (can I come in?). **Нельзя** + infinitive express prohibition: **Здесь нельзя курить** (here is prohibited smoking).",
    "The perfect verbs **отдохнуть** and **помочь** present the action as a delimited whole; their imperfect pairs **отдыхать** and **помогать** focus on the process or repetition. **Мне нельзя опоздать** means I'm not supposed to be late."
   ]
  },
